    "q-36290": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y$ at the point $A(1,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(0,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,1⟩-⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0,\\,1\\cdot {e}^{0}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =∇f(1,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
